# netsurf: a module graph load that stops progressing gives up (160/161 follow-up)
# Generated 2026-10-02
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser 370efcebdf67dc95b6b7bd4ba96ff434c939a3f4 (patch stack through 198)
#
# Patches 160/161 load <script type=module> graphs and import() graphs
# through qjs_modload pipelines. A pipeline waits, rather than fetch
# again, for any URL another load is fetching: the whole level is
# carried and re-looked at on every event-loop pass, and the compile
# phase waits the same way while another pipeline fetches. Nothing
# bounds those waits. If the other load never lands -- patch 161's
# page fetch of a module script never calls back, a level in flight
# elsewhere never resolves -- the pipeline never finishes: import()
# promises never settle, and for a prefetched static graph
# js_module_prefetch_pending() stays true, so html.c holds the page's
# conversion (and DOMContentLoaded) indefinitely, re-polling every
# 30 ms (each html content with a held conversion keeps its own poll
# chain). Every transfer the pipelines start themselves is already
# bounded (fetch_get_set gives up after BLOCKING_TIMEOUT_S); only the
# waits are not.
#
# Fix: a pipeline keeps a progress snapshot -- fetched + failed +
# card hits + compiled + levels -- and, when stepped with no transfer
# of its own running, compares it with the clock. No change for
# QJS_MODLOAD_STALL_MS and the wait can never end: what the pipeline
# still owes is registered as failed modules (the loader reports
# those exactly like a failed fetch: "could not load module '<url>'"),
# and the pipeline finishes, so its import() jobs are re-queued to
# reject and a prefetched graph stops holding the conversion. A
# module another load lands later still registers over its failed
# entry (qjs_module_register fills a source-less entry), so a slow
# graph caught by the deadline is not poisoned. A pipeline whose own
# transfer is running is never cut (fetch_get_set bounds it), and one
# that keeps fetching or compiling never trips the deadline, so no
# progressing load, however large, is killed.
#
# The bound is the engine's own: BLOCKING_TIMEOUT_S (120 s) in
# content/fetchers/curl.c is both the CURLOPT_TIMEOUT on every
# blocking transfer and the wall-clock give-up in fetch_get_set_step;
# a wedged wait is given one transfer's worth of grace.
#
# Test: JS-module-static-async / JS-module-dedupe / JS-module-async
# unchanged (MODSTATIC 5/5, MODDUP 5/5, MODASYNC 4/4) and no give-up
# logged on live graphs. 120 s is beyond a gate page's settle budget,
# so the give-up itself was exercised in a throwaway build with the
# bound at 400 ms, against a module script whose fetch never answers
# (accept-and-hold server): the log reads "LIFE module graph (async):
# gave up on http://127.0.0.1:45700/modleaf.js: no progress for
# 408 ms", where the unpatched pipeline waits forever. That page's
# conversion is still held afterwards by the script element's own
# hlcache fetch, which never completes either; bounding that is core
# fetch policy (llcache), not module code, and is not this patch.
# Full gate on the pushed stack: see docs/build-loop-state.md.
diff --git a/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/macsurf_qjs.c b/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/macsurf_qjs.c
--- a/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/macsurf_qjs.c
